welcome back to cbs saturday morning we  begin this half hour with pomp and  circumstance and a high profile visit in  hong kong as the city observes a 25-year  anniversary of independence from british  rule chinese president xi jinping made  his first trip off the mainland since  the start of the pandemic to help  celebrate the anniversary and he wasn't  shy about the prospects for china to  seize control over even more territory  elizabeth palmer reports  china's president xi jinping arrived in  hong kong by train on a victory tour to  celebrate the 25th anniversary of china  taking hong kong back  [Music]  in 1997 britain's flag came down over  the territory for the last time  marking the end of more than 150 years  of british rule  and for a while life carried on under  china's so-called one country two  systems policy in other words beijing  promised not to interfere  but slowly that changed and in 2019  violent protests erupted over a law that  would have sent people arrested in hong  kong to china for trial  in the end the demonstrators lost and  china cemented its hold  on in his keynote speech on friday xi  jinping doubled down  we must implement the principle of  patriots administering hong kong he said  outside there was heavy security and  plenty of nationalist hoopla  which to people 500 miles away on the  island of taiwan looked like a threat  in hong kong freedom and democracy have  vanished said its premier sued sen  taiwan must hold fast to its sovereignty  but it's not going to be easy china  views taiwan as its own and xi jinping  has made it clear he wants to bring it  just like hong kong under beijing's firm  control  for cbs saturday morning elizabeth  palmer tokyo
 

